        "name": "Kṣemarāja",
        "text": "Kṣemarāja concludes the first chapter with this culminating sūtra. The 'Great Lake' is a metaphor for the supreme consciousness — vast, deep, still, yet alive with infinite ripples. Sustained contemplation of this supreme reality reveals what makes mantra effective: not the mere sound, but the consciousness-potency (mantra-vīrya) that is identical with the practitioner's own awareness.\n\nThis sūtra serves as a hinge to the second chapter, which explores śāktopāya — the path that operates through mantra and the powers of consciousness. The first chapter ends by pointing to what makes that next path possible: direct experiential recognition of the source of all mantric potency."
