        "name": "Kṣemarāja",
        "text": "Kṣemarāja gives this brief sūtra a profound interpretation. The Vedic ritual tradition offers grains, ghee, and other substances into the sacrificial fire; these are the haviḥ. The Śiva Sūtras radically internalize this practice: the body itself — and by extension, the entire perceptible field — becomes the offering, given over to the fire of recognized consciousness.\n\nHe explains that this is not metaphor in the loose sense. It is the actual reorientation of one's relationship to embodiment. The body is no longer hoarded as 'my possession' or anxiously maintained as 'my identity.' It is offered up — surrendered, released — to the consciousness that is its true substance and source.\n\nKṣemarāja emphasizes that this surrender is not a loss. The body, given over, is transformed; what was previously experienced as a prison becomes recognized as an offering whose acceptance signifies the practitioner's union with the supreme. Every breath, every action of the body, becomes participation in continuous sacrificial offering."
