        "text": "Kṣemarāja explains that the 'seed' here is the source from which all manifestation arises — the supreme consciousness in its originating creative aspect. The practitioner is instructed to attend to this seed: to maintain awareness of the source from which experience continuously emerges rather than being lost in the various branches and fruits of manifestation.\n\nHe emphasizes that this is a specific contemplative discipline. Whenever something arises in experience — a thought, a feeling, a perception — the practitioner traces it back to its source rather than getting caught up in its content. With sustained practice, attention rests in the source itself, and the various manifestations are recognized as expressions of that source.\n\nKṣemarāja further notes that this practice is particularly important for the siddha who has reached the state described in the previous sūtras. The recognition of svātantrya can be undermined by losing attention to the seed and being drawn back into surface engagement. Sustained bīja-avadhāna keeps the recognition fresh."
