        "text": "Kṣemarāja gives this technical sūtra extensive treatment. The Sanskrit consonants are traditionally arranged in five classes (vargas), each beginning with a specific consonant: ka-class, ca-class, ṭa-class, ta-class, pa-class. Over each class presides a particular goddess: Māheśvarī, Kaumārī, Vaiṣṇavī, Vārāhī, Aindrī, Cāmuṇḍā, and Mahālakṣmī.\n\nHe explains that these goddesses — when their phonemic activity occurs without the practitioner's recognition — are precisely the 'mothers' of the bound being. The bound being is birthed and sustained by the unrecognized operation of the mātṛkā-cakra introduced in 1.4 and developed in 2.7.\n\nKṣemarāja further explains that this teaching is a dramatic specification of the bondage-diagnosis. The practitioner is not merely 'caught in delusion' in some abstract sense; they are caught specifically by the operation of these named goddess-powers, which structure their unconscious thought-life through the phonemic substrate of language."
