        "name": "Swami Lakshmanjoo",
        "text": "Lakshmanjoo taught this sūtra with characteristic practical wisdom. He observed that most spiritual paths address pleasure and pain by trying to reduce or eliminate them. The Kashmir Śaiva path takes a different approach: pleasure and pain are not to be eliminated but to be repositioned in one's understanding.\n\nHe often described what this repositioning looks like in practice. The realized practitioner enjoys what is enjoyable; they dislike what is unpleasant. But neither response defines them. They remain recognizably themselves through both pleasure and pain because their identity does not depend on either.\n\nLakshmanjoo also stressed that this teaching protects the realized practitioner from manipulation. The ordinary person can be controlled by promises of pleasure or threats of pain. The realized practitioner cannot be controlled in this way because they do not take pleasure and pain as their fundamental concern."
