        "name": "Kṣemarāja",
        "text": "Kṣemarāja explains that the practitioner who has genuinely succeeded in regarding pleasure and pain as external (3.33) is called the kevalin — the Solitary One. The term derives from kaivalya, the supreme isolation or uniqueness that is the goal of certain yogic traditions.\n\nHe emphasizes that the Kashmir Śaiva use of kevalin differs subtly from its use in classical Sāṃkhya-Yoga, where it refers to the absolute isolation of the puruṣa from prakṛti. In Kashmir Śaivism, the kevalin is the one who has attained perfect identification with the supreme — not isolated from the world but uniquely established in the supreme consciousness that pervades all.\n\nKṣemarāja further notes that this state is the natural continuation of the practitioner who has succeeded in repositioning pleasure and pain. Once these are no longer one's defining concerns, one stands uniquely in the supreme awareness that is one's true identity."
